// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
// Serde: the serialization contract -- `Encode`/`Decode` as interfaces, with
// the JSON engine in json::std as the first implementation.
//
// Every `finn` manifest, every LSP message, every config file is
// serialization (issue #57). Without a stated contract each tool hand-rolls
// string munging, and the shapes diverge. serde.fin is the contract; json.fin
// is the engine that answers it today.
//
// THE INTERFACES, spelled as the issue writes them:
//
//     interface Encode { fun encode(self: &Self) <string>; }
//     interface Decode { fun decode(text: string) <Outcome<Self>>; }
//
// `Outcome` is the recoverable-error spelling of issue #52 (outcome::std):
// a decode that met a bad token returns `Err(msg)` -- and the message NAMES
// what it refused -- rather than blaming a caller's data. `Outcome<Self>`
// inside the interface body RESOLVES: the module type-checks clean standalone
// and through an import (measured). So the declaration side of both
// interfaces is real language, not comment.
//
// THE DISPATCH GAP, named. The RUNTIME path below the interfaces is the
// free-function spelling the issue's own wording prescribes: "ifSelf-return
// generics can't lower yet, use free functions `decode_T(text) <Outcome<T>>`
// per concrete type and SAY SO". json::std answers with exactly that family
// (`json_decode_int`, `json_decode_str`, ...). A struct that wants the
// interface spelling today writes its `encode` method and the json family
// beside it; wiring the two into `implements <Encode>` and having a call site
// dispatch on the interface METHOD rather than on a known struct is the
// `Derive` work of issue #53. Nothing in this file fakes that dispatch --
// one dispatch that "would work if the type were named" is a trap that
// cannot be caught at a call site, and the gap is cheaper named than faked.
//
// THE CALLERS' DUTIES, spelled once:
//
//   * Strings: lib/std/strings.fin's rule, and this interface adds nothing:
//     an `encode` result is a fresh allocation the caller owns (release it
//     with `strings::free_str`; the Buffer family answers `free_string`,
//     same libc `free` underneath), and a `decode_T` returns malloc-owned
//     text the caller owns -- the copy that unescaped the source, not the
//     source's pointer. Nothing here caches, aliases, or reuses a buffer.
//   * Failed decodes return `Err` and allocate nothing on the way there
//     beyond the staging the message needs. No half-built value escapes a
//     failed decode: the caller's data either arrives whole or not at all.
//   * `blame` is never driven by Caller input. A caller passing a bad text
//     to a `decode` gets an `Outcome`, not an abort; the blame of the finc
//     diagnostics grid is for the TOOL's own state, not for user data.
//
// THE ROUND TRIP THE ISSUE NAMES, as json::std spells it:
//
//     import { json_encode_int, json_decode_int } from json::std;
//     let text <string> = json_encode_int(42);
//     let back <Outcome<int>> = json_decode_int(text);
//
// ... and the same shape for `str`/`bool`/`float`, an `[int]` array, and the
// `{string, int}`/`{string, string}` prototype maps finn manifests carry.
// The module docs under json::std say what each answers.
//
// GAPS hit while writing this file:
//
//   * Interface CONFORMANCE dispatch (`struct X implements <Encode>` plus a
//     call that reaches `encode` through the interface rather than through
//     `X`) is not built; the free functions are the runtime path and `Derive`
//     (#53) names the follow-up. The interfaces stand so the day that pass
//     lands, existing callers keep spelling the same words.
//   * A derive of the two interfaces for a user's struct is issue #53's
//     feature; the "hand-written per type v0" section of issue #57 IS the
//     state these modules document: the scalars, arrays and maps exist
//     engine-side; a caller composes a struct's encode from them.

import { Outcome } from outcome::std;

namespace std {

#[export]
pub interface Encode {
    pub fun encode(self: &Self) <string>;
}
#[export]
pub interface Decode {
    pub fun decode(text: string) <Outcome<Self>>;
}

}
